<% content_for(:title, "Rules · Cyvasse") %>

<%# The banner is the goal of the game on the current board: a Dragon with the
    enemy King at the end of its lane, captured by test/capture/home_gallery_capture.rb
    (`bin/rails cyvasse:capture_rules_hero`). A 3:1 crop for a wide screen, a
    16:9 one for a phone; the first paint, so both are preloaded. %>
<% hero = "backgrounds/rules/capture-the-king.webp"
   hero_mobile = "backgrounds/rules/capture-the-king-mobile.webp" %>
<% content_for(:head) do %>
  <link rel="preload" as="image" href="<%= image_path(hero) %>" media="(min-width: 641px)" fetchpriority="high">
  <link rel="preload" as="image" href="<%= image_path(hero_mobile) %>" media="(max-width: 640px)" fetchpriority="high">
<% end %>
<article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto rules-page">
  <%= render "pages/banner", image: hero, mobile_image: hero_mobile, title: "Rules",
                             subtitle: "The goal of Cyvasse is to capture your opponent's king." %>

  <%# The quick version the original tutorial opened with (legacy features/tutorial.js). %>
  <section id="quick-start" class="card p-5 mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-heading mb-4">The Short Version</h2>
    <dl class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-4 gap-y-3 text-sm">
      <dt class="font-semibold text-heading">Goal</dt>
      <dd class="text-secondary">Capture your opponent's king, so hide yours!</dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-heading">Setup</dt>
      <dd class="text-secondary">Each player secretly creates an opening array with their army.</dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-heading">First move</dt>
      <dd class="text-secondary">The first turn is awarded to the army whose king is closest to the front line.</dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-heading">Game</dt>
      <dd class="text-secondary">Players trade moves until a king is captured. If neither side can move, it is a draw.</dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-heading">Combat</dt>
      <dd class="text-secondary">A unit may capture an enemy if the Attacker has more or equal Strength to the Defender.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <div class="rules-prose space-y-8 mb-12">
    <section id="goal">
      <h2>Goal</h2>
      <p>The goal of Cyvasse is to capture your opponent's king.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="pregame">
      <h2>Pregame</h2>
      <p>
        Before the first move can be made, each player must place all their
        pieces on the board. Each hex on the board may hold only one unit at a
        time.
      </p>
      <p>When both players are satisfied with the arrangement of their army, the game can begin.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="who-goes-first">
      <h2>Who Goes First</h2>
      <p>
        The first move is awarded to the player whose King is closest to the
        middle row of the board. In the case of a tie, the first move is chosen
        at random. Now the game is ready to begin.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="game">
      <h2>Game</h2>
      <p>
        Now players exchange moves until one of the kings is captured. A move
        is made up of moving one of your units to a new space, or attacking an
        enemy.
      </p>
    </section>

    <%# Alex, 2026-09-25: keep the stalemate rule (Game#beginTurn, CyvasseRules::Game). %>
    <section id="no-legal-move">
      <h2>No Legal Move</h2>
      <p>
        If it is your turn and none of your units can move or attack, your turn
        passes to your opponent. The game tells you when a turn is passed.
      </p>
      <p>If neither player can move, the game ends in a draw.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="moving">
      <h2>Moving</h2>
      <p>
        All units have a Movement attribute, which explains how they may
        traverse the board. The magnitude of a unit's movement is how many
        jumps it can make in one turn. For all non-Range units, an attack
        counts as a jump.
      </p>
      <p>
        Units may not move through other units. The exception to this rule is
        the Dragon, which may fly over all units and mountains, unless they are
        enemy Range units (Crossbowman, Catapult and Trebuchet) or the enemy
        Dragon.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="combat">
      <h2>Combat</h2>
      <p>
        With the exception of the Range units, a combat is made by moving to the
        hex occupied by an opponent's piece. Range units instead choose to
        attack or move, but may not do both in one turn.
      </p>
      <p>
        A combat is fought between two pieces, an Attacker and a Defender. The
        Attacker is the unit initiating the combat; the Defender is the unit
        being attacked.
      </p>
      <p>
        When the Attacker attacks a Defender, the Strength of the Defender is
        compared to the Strength of the Attacker. The piece with the higher
        Strength wins, and in the case of a tie, the Attacker wins.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are two exceptions to this rule. The first is a Trump. Some units
        have a Trump against other units: for example, a Spearman will always
        defeat a Light Horse, because a Spearman trumps Light Horse pieces, even
        though the Light Horse has equal Strength to the Spearman. The second
        exception is that Range units can be taken by any attack, regardless of
        the Strengths of the Attacker and Defender, unless a Trump is involved.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Range unit may attack an enemy within its Range, unless a Mountain
        blocks the direct path between the Range unit and its target. Every
        Mountain blocks a shot, whichever army placed it, and no Range unit
        can shoot over one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Besides the Trebuchet and the Catapult, the King is the one unit that
        trumps the Dragon: a Dragon that comes within the King's Movement can
        be taken by it. The Dragon can still take the King.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section id="special-rules" class="mb-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading mb-2">Special Rules</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-5">Four rules worth knowing before your first game.</p>
    <%= render "pages/special_rules" %>
  </section>

  <section id="units" class="mb-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading mb-2">Units</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Each player's army has <%= Rulebook.army_size %> pieces:
      <%= Rulebook.army_unit_count %> units of <%= Rulebook.unit_kind_count %> kinds,
      and <%= Rulebook.mountain_count %> Mountains. Each kind of unit has its own
      attributes, which it uses to interact with the map and enemy units. Below
      is a description of every unit and its attributes, grouped by class.
    </p>
    <div class="mb-6"><%= render "skins/toggle" %></div>

    <div class="space-y-8">
      <% @unit_classes.each do |unit_class| %>
        <section id="class-<%= unit_class.name.downcase %>" class="unit-class">
          <h3 class="unit-class-title text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-muted mb-4"><%= unit_class.name %></h3>
          <%# Thirds on a wide screen, halves on a tablet, one a row on a phone;
              a third is narrow enough that the card stacks its stats (.unit-card). %>
          <div class="unit-grid grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
            <% unit_class.units.each do |unit| %>
              <%= render "pages/unit_card", unit:, id: "unit-#{unit.slug}" %>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </section>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="rule-changes" class="card p-5 mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-heading mb-1">Rule Changes</h2>
    <p class="text-muted text-sm mb-4">The unit stats above include every change below.</p>
    <% [ [ "changes-2026", "September 29, 2026", Rulebook::CHANGES_2026 ],
         [ "changes-2015", "April 14, 2015", Rulebook::CHANGES_2015 ] ].each do |id, date, changes| %>
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-heading mt-4 mb-2">Implemented on <%= date %></h3>
      <ul id="<%= id %>" class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-secondary">
        <% changes.each do |change| %>
          <li><%= change %></li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    <% end %>
  </section>
</article>
